Mr. Pitcher,

I have read everything you have put your name to in my matter, and the 
one thing you did not.

Your title is Public Records Counsel. The man who holds it is trusted to 
tell Oregon the truth about its records law, to apply that law to his 
own department as he would to any other, and to put his name to what he 
decides. You were a criminal prosecutor for eleven years and handled the 
Washington County District Attorney's public records for five. You were 
introduced to the Oregon Sunshine Committee as the Department of 
Justice's counsel on records. At the Attorney General's Public Law 
Conference, you are the one at the front of the room teaching Oregon's 
public bodies how to answer a records request.

Those three trusts are the whole of the role.

You were trusted to tell Oregon the truth about its records law. On 
October 15, 2026, you told it that the file your department keeps on a 
citizen's letters to his government is his private interest.

You were trusted to apply that law to your own department as you would 
to any other. You answered for the Attorney General's orders on a 
citizen's petitions against other agencies, and then priced your own 
department's file on him at $599.

You were trusted to put your name to what you decide. Your explanations 
carry your name, with no title beneath it. Your denial of my fee waiver 
on October 15, 2026 carries no name at all. The orders went out over 
another man's signature, with the initials "DEP:pjn" at the foot. The 
more a document decides, the less of you is on it.

The archive of this matter keeps a page for every official who acted in 
it, with two lines beside each name: the role the title promises, and 
the function the record shows. Yours is there, and the second line 
reads: "Guards his department's file on the volunteer from both sides of 
the records law."

If either line is wrong, you are the one person in this matter who can 
correct it, in a letter you sign.

*Robert Samuel White*
